{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a soft pink snow settling on temple stones and quiet streets. No loud alarms, no rush. Just the whisper of wind through bamboo groves and the distant chime of a temple bell.\n\nI spent mornings sipping matcha in hidden teahouses, where the bitterness was balanced by the sweetness of wagashi sweets shaped like maple leaves. In Nishiki Market, I tried tako tamago—octopus stuffed with quail egg—and didn’t regret it. (I still dream about the grilled yakitori, though.)\n\nOne afternoon, I wandered Arashiyama’s bamboo forest. The towering stalks swayed like green giants, their rustling a natural lullaby. I met an elderly woman selling hand-painted fans. She smiled, said “kawaii,” and pressed one into my hand. No price. Just a nod.\n\nTokyo was a shock of light and motion. Shibuya’s scramble crossing felt like being swept into a movie. But my favorite moment? Sitting alone on a rooftop in Shinjuku, eating a bento box as neon signs blinked above me—pink, blue, gold—and the city hummed like a living thing.\n\nI took the bullet train to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was silent, heavy with memory. A single origami crane on the memorial bench—left by someone, maybe years ago—still held its shape. I folded another and placed it beside it.\n\nOn my last night, I stayed in a ryokan. Tatami mats, a hot bath under the stars, and a kimono that felt like wearing a cloud. The owner served miso soup with a whisper: “Oishii desu ne?” (“Isn’t it delicious?”)\n\nI didn’t just visit Japan. I breathed it.\n\nI left with a heart fuller than my suitcase—and a promise to return before the next sakura season.", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token"